.TH TPUT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
tput \- initialize a terminal or query its capabilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tput
.RB [ \-T
.IR type ]
.I clear
.br
.B tput
.RB [ \-T
.IR type ]
.I init
.br
.B tput
.RB [ \-T
.IR type ]
.I reset
.br
.B tput
.RB [ \-T
.IR type ]
.I capname
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tput
looks up and prints the control sequence for a named terminal
capability, or the numeric value of one, for the terminal type given
by
.B \-T
.IR type ,
or by
.B $TERM
if
.B \-T
is not given, or (if
.B $TERM
is also unset or empty)
.RB \` dumb \' .
.PP
Terminal capabilities are looked up in a fixed, built\-in table
covering five terminal types \(em
.BR xterm ", " xterm\-256color ", " vt100 ", " ansi ", and " dumb
\(em rather than read from a system terminfo or termcap database: this
implementation runs in environments (a from\-scratch bootstrap, and
Windows) that cannot be relied on to have one installed at all. A
.B \-T
.I type
or
.B $TERM
value naming any other terminal type is refused.
.PP
.B clear
is both a standard operand and a capability name, and shares the same
table entry either way.
.B init
and
.B reset
always succeed and write nothing, for every terminal type in the
table: none of them define an initialization or reset sequence, which
is a legitimate, specification\-permitted outcome, not a lookup
failure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-T " type"
Use
.I type
in place of
.BR $TERM .
May also be written attached, as
.BI \-T type
with no space. Takes precedence over
.B $TERM
when both would otherwise apply.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.B clear
Print the sequence that clears the screen and homes the cursor.
.TP
.B init
Write nothing; always succeeds.
.TP
.B reset
Write nothing; always succeeds.
.TP
.B cols
Print the terminal's width, in columns. If standard output is itself a
terminal, its actual current width is queried and printed; otherwise
(or if that query fails) the selected terminal type's fixed table
value is printed. All five built\-in terminal types define 80.
.TP
.B lines
Print the terminal's height, in rows, the same way
.B cols
does for width. The
.B dumb
terminal type defines no line count at all, so
.B lines
against it (with standard output not itself a live terminal to query)
prints nothing and fails.
.TP
.BR bold ", " smso ", " rmso ", " smul ", " rmul ", " rev ", " sgr0
Print the sequence to, respectively: enter bold, enter standout mode,
exit standout mode, enter underline, exit underline, enter reverse
video, and turn off all attributes. Not defined for the
.B dumb
terminal type, against which each of these fails and prints nothing.
.TP
.BI "cup " row " " col
Print the sequence to move the cursor to
.I row
and
.IR col ,
each given as a non\-negative integer, 0\-based (the first row and
column are both 0). Not defined for the
.B dumb
terminal type.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
No general terminfo parameter\-string interpreter is implemented;
.B cup
is the only parameterized capability recognized, and is handled as a
special case. There is no
.B longname
operand, and no
.B $COLUMNS / $LINES
environment override for
.BR cols / lines ;
only a live terminal query or the built\-in table's fixed value is
ever consulted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The selected terminal type's table entry does not define the
requested capability.
.TP
.B 2
A required operand is missing, an argument to
.B \-T
is missing, or a parameterized capability
.RB ( cup )
was given the wrong number of arguments.
.TP
.B 3
.B \-T
.I type
or
.B $TERM
names a terminal type outside the built\-in table.
.TP
.B 4
The operand is neither
.BR clear ", " init ", or " reset
nor a recognized capability name.
.TP
.B 5
A write failure occurred while printing a capability's sequence.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stty (1),
.BR tty (1)
